  "preamble": "Retroflexion of the dental/alveolar nasal [n] to the retroflex nasal [ɳ]. In Sanskrit it is the nati rule: n becomes ṇ after r, ṛ or ṣ in the same word, which also reshaped inherited words (pūrṇa, uṣṇa). In Middle Indo-Aryan (Prakrit) single intervocalic n became ṇ generally; western and northern descendants such as Punjabi, Marathi and Gujarati keep the retroflex nasal (pāṇī 'water'), while Hindi has restored dental n (pānī).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Retroflexion, Assimilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Sanskrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *pl̥h₁nós 'full' (Latin plēnus)",
          "to": "पूर्ण pūrṇa /puːɾ.ɳɐ/",
          "note": "The nasal following r is retroflex ṇ by the nati rule."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *Hušnás 'hot'",
          "to": "उष्ण uṣṇá /uʂ.ɳɐ́/",
          "note": "After retroflex ṣ the following nasal is retroflexed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
